00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.920
Nicole: Threat status is the current
level of malicious cyber activity and

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.029
the potential for damage or disruption
to a computer network or system.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.376
Threat status can include information
regarding attacks from malware or DoS

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.850
and will determine whether software
needs to be updated and or extra training

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:22.220
is needed within an organisation or
business to bring everyone up to speed.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.420
The higher the threat status
the greater the threat.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:29.569
Organisations and businesses may
have their own system for labelling

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.090
this by using colours or numbers etc.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:35.780
Abraham: Threat modelling can be
used to determine a threat status

00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:39.440
and identify potential security
threats and any vulnerabilities.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.010
They can also help to determine
how serious the threat is and

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.350
what it’s impact would be.

00:00:45.099 --> 00:00:48.469
So how do security professionals
determine threat status?

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:51.670
Let’s look at some of the
processes that they can use.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:56.370
Nicole: Gathering threat intelligence
using OSINT and other reliable sources

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:00.830
to gather information on current threats,
vulnerabilities tools and processes

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.180
and the motivations for threat actors.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.489
Keeping up to date with all
assets that are in use within

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.170
the business or organisation.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.180
This can help them track down
any flaws or vulnerabilities

00:01:12.209 --> 00:01:13.649
that may need to be addressed.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.390
Therefore, it is important to
get alerts if any unauthorised

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.110
downloads are made by staff.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.209
Abraham: Keeping up to date with the
ability to mitigate an attack by making

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:27.699
sure the technology is able to counter an
attack, such as upgrading the firewall.

00:01:28.209 --> 00:01:32.929
Doing regular risk assessments especially
after expanding any assets, to determine

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.960
how attacks may take place and develop
plans for addressing any vulnerabilities.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.769
Nicole: Threat mapping the potential
paths of threats and how attackers

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.630
may get from one resource to another.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:47.920
This can help identify weak
areas and where extra resources

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.289
or training may be needed.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:53.170
After a risk has been assessed
the current threat status is

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.870
determined and then recommendations
and actions will take place.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.080
The exact recommendations
will always depend on the

00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:01.800
type and level of the threat.

